name: Mastodon work session - audit the account-record contract before a rework
repo: mastodon
contract_symbol: Account
contract_file: app/models/account.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 9 - a RE-QUESTION.
# It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at the real wall,
# unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta is claimed and
# none has been measured on this shape.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TRAJECTORY, EVERY CYCLE, AND WHICH SIDE OF THE WINDOW IT FELL ON
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The window is BOTH conditions at once: baseline `dependents` at or below 0.50 AND sense
# at least +0.50 above it. FAR = the baseline cleared the question itself. NEAR = the
# baseline was held down but our arm did not reach either.
#
#   cycle 1  mastodon.yaml.class-call-sites.bak   base 0.8125  sense 1.000  d +0.1875  FAR
#   cycle 2  mastodon.yaml.line-not-file.bak      base 0.158   sense 0.158  d  0.000   NEAR
#            (baseline half CLEARED; the sense arm never called blast on the class)
#   cycle 3  mastodon.yaml.table-altitude.bak     base 0.091   sense 0.091  d  0.000   NEAR
#            (both arms audited the accounts TABLE and agreed on the wrong question)
#   cycle 4  mastodon.yaml.names-the-class.bak    base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#   cycle 5  mastodon.yaml.not-the-call-form.bak  base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#   cycle 6  (author phase, `Account#local?`)     no run                              n/a
#            NO-ANCHOR reached from a per-row grep census - a screen used as a gate, which
#            laws.md forbids in any form. It decided nothing and is not counted.
#   cycle 7  mastodon.yaml.line-pin-no-shape.bak  base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#   cycle 8  mastodon.yaml.inherited-helpers.bak  base 1.000   sense 1.000  d  0.000   FAR
#            (the ANCHOR MOVE: `Account` retired for `ApplicationController`, whose gold was
#            19 of 19 token-DARK files. The baseline read the base file once, printed the
#            inherited helper names, grepped those, and took 19 of 19 in 21 calls.)
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM: the FAR side, six of the eight runs, and by a full
# 0.50. Intended landing: baseline at or below ~0.45, sense at or above ~0.95.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE EIGHT CYCLES HAVE ALREADY CLOSED - do not re-run these levers
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  a. REWORDING THE MECHANISM. Named, withheld, re-named: cycles 4, 5 and 7 measured
#     1.000, 1.000, 1.000. Withholding the call shapes cost the baseline four extra Bash
#     calls and bought zero rows (cycle 7's own report).
#  b. TOKEN-DARK GOLD. Cycle 8 built the strongest possible version - 19 of 19 gold files
#     with ZERO occurrences of the anchor's name - and the baseline took all 19. Darkness
#     of the CLASS name does not help when the dependents share any small enumerable token.
#  c. MOVING THE ANCHOR. Also cycle 8. Same number, 1.000. The anchor is not the variable.
#  d. PINNING THE LINE RATHER THAN THE FILE. Closed by the scorer, not by a run.
#     `bench/lib/gold.py:_cited` credits a gold path followed by ANY line number; the
#     specific line is never compared to gold's. So "which LINE, not which file" (cycle 7's
#     whole thesis) is not a thing the metric can see. The metric is: WHICH FILES did the
#     answer name, with some line attached. Nothing else.
#  e. TRIAGE / PRECISION FRAMINGS. `bench/lib/scorer.py` dropped gold_f1; recall only, so
#     an arm that dumps every candidate pays nothing for over-citing.
#
# What that leaves is a question about which FILES an arm can still be naming when its
# budget is half spent. That is the one variable eight cycles never touched.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS - the two levers, both untested, stated so the next phase can measure them
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEVER 1 - SESSION POSITION, MADE TESTABLE IN TWO STEPS. laws.md carries this as a
# hypothesis under test, not established: this repo's ONE banked win (+0.53, baseline 7 of
# 23, sense 19 of 23) is the SAME occurrence-list kind as the eight failures, and the
# difference on the record is that it sits at step 4 of a 7-step session, so both arms
# arrive with wall and context already spent, while all eight probes asked it at step 2 of
# 2 with the wall fresh. Cycle 7's report concluded a two-step probe therefore CANNOT
# measure it. That is only true while step 1 is cheap orientation, and in all eight cycles
# it was. Here step 1 is deliberately expensive and it is the COST, not the wording, that
# is the experiment: the record class's body is assembled from seventeen files at load
# time (540 lines in the class, 1145 more across sixteen mixed-in modules), and step 1 asks
# for the assembly map with file:line for every part. An arm cannot answer it from the
# class file alone and cannot answer it from memory - see MEMORIZATION below, where the
# probe recites member NAMES at 0.387 but no defining file for any of them.
# NOTE the honest risk, stated before the run: laws.md also says squeezing the WALL only
# voids arms. This does not squeeze the wall - both arms keep their normal budget - it
# spends it on real work first. If the baseline arrives at step 2 and still closes at
# 1.000, the session-position hypothesis is answered NO for a two-step shape and the next
# cycle must stop proposing it.
#
# LEVER 2 - GOLD FROM THE SINGLETON PERIPHERY ONLY. Every previous gold set mixed dense
# areas (app/services at 35 direct callers, app/models at 31, app/controllers/api/v1 at 13)
# with sparse ones, and the dense areas are where a token sweep's output starts. All 16
# rows below are drawn from areas the shown blast payload reports at ONE OR TWO direct
# callers - validators, schedulers, system checks, metric dimensions, presenters, importers,
# form objects, serializer decorators. Cycle 1 is the only measurement that bears on this
# and it points the right way: its three misses were the tail of a 100+ hit list, and its
# own report called them "attention drop-off at the tail", not a blind spot. The bet is that
# drop-off at the tail is what a spent budget amplifies, and that the blast payload is
# order-free and paid once. That is a bet. It is not established and it is not evidence.
#
# THE SENTENCE PLAN 01 ASKS FOR, ANSWERED HONESTLY. This is KIND ONE. The answer to step 2
# IS a list of the places a name appears; every one of the 16 gold files contains the
# literal token `Account`, and one grep prints all of them. That is stated plainly rather
# than dressed up, per the plan's own instruction. What is under test is not the shape of
# the answer, it is whether an arm still reaches the tail of that list after paying for
# step 1.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CONTRACT AND ITS SEAM PROFILE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `Account`, app/models/account.rb - the record that both local users and remote actors are
# rows in, the model everything in this codebase hangs off. Kept, per ITERATE ON THE
# QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR, and because cycle 8 measured that moving it changes nothing.
#
#   python3 bench/lib/anchor_rank.py $CLONE --top 20
#     Account   class   dep_files 206   app/models/account.rb      <- rank 1 of 20
#
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Account --file app/models/account.rb
#     app caller files 107; SCATTER (dirs) 34; 'Account' in 257 app files;
#     PRECISION=0.42 (lower = grep-hostile); VERDICT "SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY".
#     Re-run this cycle, not carried over from a previous header. It is the lowest
#     precision profiled on this repo across nine cycles. Compared on the same command
#     this cycle: ApplicationPolicy PRECISION 0.53 / SCATTER 10 dirs, TagManager
#     PRECISION 0.53 / SCATTER 22 dirs; Serializer and BaseService both report SCATTER 0
#     dirs with "RESOLVER-GAP?", so neither can serve a gold row at all.
#   Calibration on the record: 0.078 preceded a +1.00 cell, 0.78 was a deliberate control
#   tie, ~0.94 handed a baseline 17 of 18 rows in one grep. 0.42 RANKS mid. It never kills.
#
#   python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#     0 candidate(s) above the bar - all twelve anchors at 0 rows / 0 files / 0 vias.
#   So "what HOLDS X" is off the menu repo-wide, for the third sweep running. Kind one is
#   not a preference here, it is the only kind this stack can serve.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE ARM IS SHOWN, AT MCP DEFAULTS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE \
#     '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Account","file":"app/models/account.rb"}}]'
#     risk high, risk_factors ["200 direct callers"], affected_symbols 336,
#     affected_files 61, production_affected 60, indirect_callers [] (no second hop),
#     direct_callers_by_area spans 30 areas.
# EVERY one of the 16 `dependents` rows below appears in that shown payload:
#   python3 bench/lib/gold_confidence_check.py $YAML Account --repo $CLONE \
#     --group dependents --file app/models/account.rb
#     gold rows: 16   shown: 16   missing: 0    PASS
# The payload names the enclosing `def` and NOT the dependency line, so every line below was
# opened and read by hand this cycle; the relations are written from that output, never from
# a script tally, and `gold_audit.py verify` passes on 22 of 22 hand-audited rows.
#
# ONE FACT ABOUT THE FIRST CALL, which the next phase should expect in the transcripts.
# `sense_blast {"symbol":"Account"}` with NO file returns no dependents at all - it returns
# a disambiguation object: `"ambiguous": true, "matches": 13`, with a hint naming
# app/models/account.rb, because thirteen symbols in this repo are called `Account` (the
# React component, the CLI maintenance class, and ten migration-local shims). The sense arm
# must pass `file` to be shown anything. Cycle 7's transcript shows it doing exactly that
# unprompted, so this is a documented shape of the run, not a hazard - but a sense arm that
# stops at the ambiguity reply scores zero, and that is now a thing to check for rather than
# to infer from a low number.
#
# THE INVERSE-FREQUENCY RANKING (plan step 0c) contributed nothing to this gold set and is
# recorded so the next cycle does not re-run it expecting more:
#   python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py mastodon $VDIR/results/claude-opus-5/validation
#   10 runs, baseline x5 + sense x5 - but that root is the BANKED `Status` cell, a different
#   scenario (sha256:f576efe) with different gold. It reports 10 free rows and 2 unreachable
#   spec rows ON THAT CELL. There is no per-row citation history for any `Account` gold row
#   at n>=5 on one wording, because no `Account` wording has been benched twice. Nothing was
#   dropped or kept on its authority.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Account, this cycle):
#   ok false, recall 0.387, hits_n 36, truth_n 93, samples [0.0, 0.258, 0.387]
#   reason: "closed-book recall 0.387 (36 members named) >= 0.3 - recited"
#   hits include: USERNAME_RE, MENTION_RE, INSTANCE_ACTOR_ID, NOTE_LENGTH_LIMIT,
#   STALE_THRESHOLD, AUTOMATED_ACTOR_TYPES, from_text, acct, discoverable, ...
# The class's own member list is recited. Acted on, not noted:
#   - NOT ONE gold row's credit is a member name. Every one is a line in a DIFFERENT file,
#     and the probe produces no file for any member it recites.
#   - Step 1 is therefore framed as WHERE each part of the surface is DEFINED, across the
#     seventeen files, not WHAT the surface contains. Reciting names does not answer it.
#   - Every gold file's last commit is 2026-06-12, after the model snapshot (churn-dated
#     this cycle with `git log -1 --format=%ad`, 16 of 16 dependents plus 6 of 6 contract).
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the record class that both local people and
  remote actors are stored as - the one every other part of this codebase reaches for
  when it needs to know who someone is. The rework will change what that class hands
  back to its callers, so before you touch it you have to know two things: how the
  class is assembled today, and what across the codebase would have to change with it.
  For everything you name, give an exact file:line a teammate could jump straight to,
  plus one phrase on why it matters. A filename with no line number does not count, and
  a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. The
    class in question is not written in one place: most of what it offers its callers is
    contributed at load time by modules mixed into it, and the file that declares the class
    mostly just lists them. Produce the assembly map. For EVERY module mixed in, give the
    file:line where it is mixed in, the file:line where that module is defined, and one
    phrase on which part of the class's surface it contributes - the query scopes, the
    lookup entry points, the relations to other records, the counters, the moderation
    states, the search surface, and so on. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework
    would have to preserve. Naming a module without saying where it is defined does not
    count, and neither does listing what the class offers without saying which file
    contributes it. Give file:line for everything you name.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '8'
    required: true
    description: Modules located at their definition site, not just named
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the account-record contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
    first part already cost you. Find EVERY place in the codebase that depends on the record
    class, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be exhaustive and group them by area.
    The dependents you will find first are the ones that are about people - the parts of the
    system whose whole job is this record. Those are the easy half and they are not what
    breaks quietly. The half that breaks quietly consults the record to settle a question
    about something else entirely: it is doing some other job, it reaches for this class
    once in the middle of it, and it sits alone in a corner of the tree that has nothing
    else like it. Do not stop where dependents are dense. For each dependent give file:line
    and one phrase on how it uses the record class. A filename with no line number does not
    count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Named dependents outside the areas that are about the record itself
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the account record. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (6, NON-SCORING anchors): the class file and five of the modules mixed into
#     it. These are step 1's targets. Both arms reach them and they do NOT score; they are
#     here so the scored group is not diluted by the obvious centre, and so step 1's cost is
#     charged without step 1 giving anything away.
#   - `dependents` (16, THE DISCRIMINATOR): one row per FILE, across 14 distinct directories,
#     every one of them an area the shown blast payload reports at ONE OR TWO direct callers.
#     No row is from app/services (35 callers), app/models (31) or app/controllers/api/v1
#     (13) - the dense areas a token sweep's output opens with. Every row appears in the
#     shown blast payload above and every line below was read by hand.
# Stated plainly: all 16 files contain the literal token `Account`. This gold is NOT curated
# for darkness - cycle 8 measured that darkness does not help - it is curated for distance
# from where an enumeration starts.
gold:
- {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [app/models/account.rb], relation: "app/models/account.rb:91 `include Attachmentable # Load prior to Avatar & Header concerns` - the contract under rework; lines 91-112 are the twenty-two mixin lines that assemble the class"}
- {id: c:associations, group: contract, match: [app/models/concerns/account/associations.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/associations.rb:3 `module Account::Associations` - contributes every relation the record has to other records"}
- {id: c:finder, group: contract, match: [app/models/concerns/account/finder_concern.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/finder_concern.rb:6 `class_methods do` - contributes the class-level lookup entry points callers reach the record through"}
- {id: c:interactions, group: contract, match: [app/models/concerns/account/interactions.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/interactions.rb:3 `module Account::Interactions` - contributes the follow/block/mute relations and their predicates"}
- {id: c:search, group: contract, match: [app/models/concerns/account/search.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/search.rb:6 `DISALLOWED_TSQUERY_CHARACTERS` - contributes the text-search surface and its ranking SQL"}
- {id: c:counters, group: contract, match: [app/models/concerns/account/counters.rb], relation: "app/models/concerns/account/counters.rb:6 `ALLOWED_COUNTER_KEYS = %i(statuses_count following_count followers_count).freeze` - contributes the cached counter surface"}
- {id: d:note-length-validator, group: dependents, match: [app/validators/note_length_validator.rb], relation: "app/validators/note_length_validator.rb:19 `new_text.gsub!(Account::MENTION_RE, '@\\2')` - a length validator strips mentions using the record's mention regexp before counting graphemes"}
- {id: d:self-destruct-scheduler, group: dependents, match: [app/workers/scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb], relation: "app/workers/scheduler/self_destruct_scheduler.rb:54 `@inboxes ||= Account.inboxes` - the instance-shutdown scheduler collects delivery targets from a class-level scope"}
- {id: d:media-privacy-check, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/admin/system_check/media_privacy_check.rb], relation: "app/lib/admin/system_check/media_privacy_check.rb:102 `Account.representative.media_attachments.create!(file: tmp_file)` - an admin system check uploads a probe file as the instance's representative record"}
- {id: d:space-usage-dimension, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/admin/metrics/dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb], relation: "app/lib/admin/metrics/dimension/space_usage_dimension.rb:44 `Account.sum(Arel.sql('COALESCE(avatar_file_size, 0) + COALESCE(header_file_size, 0)'))` - a storage-dashboard dimension aggregates avatar and header bytes off the record"}
- {id: d:serializer-model-name, group: dependents, match: [app/serializers/rest/account_serializer.rb], relation: "app/serializers/rest/account_serializer.rb:29 `Account.model_name` - a SimpleDelegator decorator forwards the record's model name so serialization keys stay stable"}
- {id: d:account-batch-form, group: dependents, match: [app/models/form/account_batch.rb], relation: "app/models/form/account_batch.rb:69 `Account.where(id: account_ids)` - a moderation batch form resolves checked ids back to records"}
- {id: d:instance-presenter, group: dependents, match: [app/presenters/instance_presenter.rb], relation: "app/presenters/instance_presenter.rb:54 `User.confirmed.joins(:account).merge(Account.without_suspended).count` - the public instance profile counts users through a record scope"}
- {id: d:json-ld-helper, group: dependents, match: [app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/json_ld_helper.rb:233 `on_behalf_of ||= Account.representative` - federation fetches fall back to the instance's representative record to sign requests"}
- {id: d:statuses-index-importer, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/importer/statuses_index_importer.rb], relation: "app/lib/importer/statuses_index_importer.rb:74 `Poll.joins(:votes).where(votes: { account: Account.local }).select(polls: [:id, :status_id])` - the search reindexer restricts poll votes to local records"}
- {id: d:action-log-filter, group: dependents, match: [app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb], relation: "app/models/admin/action_log_filter.rb:114 `account = Account.find_or_initialize_by(id: value)` - the audit-log filter resolves a target id to a record so it can match polymorphic targets"}
- {id: d:friends-of-friends, group: dependents, match: [app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb], relation: "app/models/account_suggestions/friends_of_friends_source.rb:10 `Account.find_by_sql([<<~SQL.squish, { id: account.id, limit: limit }])` - a follow-suggestion source instantiates records from raw SQL"}
- {id: d:extractor, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/extractor.rb], relation: "app/lib/extractor.rb:28 `text.scan(Account::MENTION_RE) do |screen_name, _|` - the text extractor finds mentions with the record's mention regexp"}
- {id: d:entity-cache, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/entity_cache.rb], relation: "app/lib/entity_cache.rb:15 `Account.select(:id, :username, :domain, :url).find_remote(username, domain)` - the entity cache memoises a narrowed remote lookup"}
- {id: d:feed-manager, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/feed_manager.rb], relation: "app/lib/feed_manager.rb:21 `Account.joins(:user).merge(User.signed_in_recently).find_each(&block)` - timeline maintenance iterates recently-active records in batches"}
- {id: d:signature-verification, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb], relation: "app/controllers/concerns/signature_verification.rb:48 `signed_request_actor.is_a?(Account) ? signed_request_actor : nil` - HTTP signature verification type-tests the resolved actor against the record class"}
- {id: d:ap-create-delivered, group: dependents, match: [app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb], relation: "app/lib/activitypub/activity/create.rb:170 `@delivered_to_account ||= Account.find(@options[:delivered_to_account_id])` - inbound federated posts resolve the delivery target before inserting into a home feed"}
